Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Boiler Sobs Rum
- Story & Context: At the Longyearbyen Rail Terminal during the first blizzard of polar night, steam-fitter Jonatan Vega attempted to thaw a frozen locomotive engine with fifty gallons of industrial Jamaican cane alcohol, only for the boiler to rupture and coat the frost-heaved tracks in flaming, syrupy wash. As the runaway train coasted into the drift, its shuddering brake pistons wheezed out the erratic four-beats-per-minute rhythm of an arctic squirrel slowing its blood for winter, inspiring Vega to capture the dying thuds on an overdriven baritone guitar. The resulting recording preserved the engine's final dying mechanical tremor, immortalized when Vega screamed into the cabin mic: "The rails are sweating golden grease, but our blood just stopped."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A massive steam locomotive encrusted in thick glacial rime, derailed on arctic tracks and weeping glowing, amber rum into deep drifts of snow under a searing, impossible tropical noon sun; floating sugarcane stalks and burning ice crystals, dramatic surreal oil painting style.

Track 2: "Which Platform Freezes First?"
- Story & Context: Astrid Baptiste, the night dispatcher at Svalbard Station, spent thirty hours locked in her glass booth watching her breath mist over the timetable monitors while nursing a Thermos of sour passionfruit tea. In a state of sleep-deprived torpor, she deliberately derailed the evening express freight by overriding the track switches, driven mad by the rhythmic disparity between her Caribbean memories and the glacial hush outside her window. Over the station loudspeaker, she whispered her confession directly into a fuzz pedal, timing her syllables to the erratic hibernation cycle of a wood frog buried beneath the permafrost, singing softly: "Forgive my delay, darling, my lungs are packed with warm sweet ice."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An abandoned, frost-shattered arctic railway platform littered with discarded neon lei garlands and frozen fruit, illuminated by an eerie warm amber station lantern; mist curling around steel train tracks that vanish into a whiteout blizzard, moody cinematic atmosphere.

Track 3: Møt-Bacchanal at Kilometer 80
- Story & Context: Out at the unmapped terminal where the permafrost rail line dead-ends into the Barents Sea, local myth tells of an uncrewed handcar that travels only one tie per hour, moving strictly to the low-frequency heartbeat of a sleeping maternal polar bear beneath the snow. When explorer Magnus Lindqvist found the rusted handcar in mid-August, he discovered a solar-powered tape machine continuously looping a wall-of-sound doom chord laced with glistening, high-register marimba arpeggios that caused migratory birds to dive into the slush. Scratched into the handcar's iron brake lever was a single sentence that became the scene's ultimate mantra: "No conductor rings the bell when the summer sleeps ten feet under."
